{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:e2a3cd00c5b5974deb99b2566520027fbcc2b7e84abd9f94fa84927469b80bbf","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.8},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.8},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.85}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR ADOPTED: Historian, P10 0.4 / P50 1.6 / P90 3.0, decomposed as r* 1.00 + real term premium 0.75 - repression wedge 0.30 = 1.45, rounded up to 1.6. MANDATED DECOMPOSITION, and the key observation first: the three Wave-1 seats AGREE on r* (0.90-1.00) and on the wedge (-0.25 to -0.30) and disagree ONLY on the real term premium (0.75 Historian / 1.10 Scout / 1.30 Fermi). So this question is a term-premium question, and that is where all my updating goes. COMPONENT 1, r* = 0.95%. Fermi's GDP-weighted 2026 base is 0.93% (US 1.3 at 0.48, euro area 0.5, Japan 0.15, UK 1.0, France 0.5, Italy 0.3, Canada 1.5) and his signed change list nets to ZERO bps: +5 pure demography (life-cycle dissaving +25 netted against the still-dominant longevity saving leg -20), +35 public dissaving and rollover volume, +10 AI/grid loanable-funds demand (a $0.7-1.1T/yr claim on ~$25T of world gross saving, haircut by AI-Q4's 66%), -20 precautionary saving weighted by AI-Q5's 38%, -10 cognitive-compression income effect per BIS Box C, -20 Chinese and OECD glut recycling. CANDIDATE EVIDENCE: the Fed hiked 16 Sep 2026 and holds 3.75-4.00% with hawkish dissents. As evidence for r* >= 1.5% the LR is ~1.2:1 — a 3.75-4.00% nominal against 3.1% core is a ~0.7-0.9% real policy rate, which is a BEHIND-THE-CURVE signal, not a high-r* signal. BELOW 2:1, DISCOUNTED. r* stays at the agreed 0.95. COMPONENT 2, real term premium = +1.15%, and this is my one genuinely strong update. EVIDENCE: DFII10 2.60% (30 Oct 2026, FRED DFII10 — verifies the locked 2A figure exactly) and 2.62% on 2 Nov; DFII30 3.06-3.08%, so the real 10s30s slope is ~+45bp and POSITIVE; DGS10 4.94%/4.91%; T10YIE 2.34% falling to 2.29-2.30%; JGB 30y all-time high 4.235% intraday 5 Oct 2026, close 3.895% on 27 Oct, 10y JGB peak ~3.12% on 1 and 6 Oct. UK 30y gilts at 1998 highs: UNVERIFIED AT THE SCOUT'S HORIZON, zero weight. ACM term premium: NO October 2026 value found; nearest verifiable 0.73% at 30 Apr 2026, ~0.83% at 15 May 2026, CEIC 0.782 for January 2026; the 0.96% dated 18 Sep 2026 is KIM-WRIGHT not ACM, the 0.03% June 2026 figure names no model, and the 1.76% year-end figure is \"a statistical projection, not an observed estimate.\" I therefore do NOT lean on an ACM level. LIKELIHOOD RATIO: P(positive real 10s30s slope of +45bp AND a synchronised record-high JGB long end | real 10y TP averages >= 100bp over 2032-36) / P(same | TP < 100bp) = 0.55/0.22 = 2.5:1. JUSTIFICATION, since this is the one LR carrying my answer: three independent sovereigns repricing duration simultaneously is hard to generate from a US-idiosyncratic fiscal story or from noise, and Japan's participation specifically rules out the high-marginal-product-of-capital alternative reading of a high real rate. The counter-case — that a transient duration selloff can precede mean reversion, as 2013-14 preceded a near-zero 2015-21 premium — is real and is why the denominator is 0.22 rather than 0.10. ARITHMETIC: Historian's prior (centre 0.75, sigma 0.70) gives P(TP >= 1.00) = P(z >= 0.357) = 0.36, odds 0.563; x 2.5 = 1.407 -> p = 0.585; inverting with sigma 0.62 gives a posterior centre of 1.00 + 0.215(0.62) = 1.13%. I then ADD ONLY +20bp of further widening for rollover volume and convenience-yield erosion rather than Fermi's +38bp, because of the Historian's double-counting argument, which I accept in full (below). TP = +1.15%, landing between Scout's 1.10 and Fermi's 1.30. COMPONENT 3, repression wedge = -0.28%, treated as a priced policy option: all three seats independently put P(material) at 0.40-0.45 and I adopt 0.42, with Fermi's weighting logic — the US is ~half the G7 weight and the least likely explicit adopter, while Japan's existing ~100bp wedge (BoJ holding ~45% of JGBs) at 0.10 weight is already baked into the aggregate. SUM: 0.95 + 1.15 - 0.28 = 1.82, reported 1.8. RESPECTING THE 2A DISTINCTION: \"aging worsens global (r - g) by ~+1.0pp EVEN WHERE a frictionless r* eases\" is a term-premium and fiscal-arithmetic claim. My r* nets to flat-to-easing and my CQ3 of 1.85% sits ~1.0-1.1pp below the no-aging counterfactual (~2.56% Fermi, ~2.9-3.2% Historian), which over-delivers the entire +1.0pp on the g side alone. Booking it again as a higher r* would move the same quantity twice; a panelist who does so lands at 2.8-3.0% and is too high by 60-90bp. MEAN-REVERSION ASSUMPTION STATED EXPLICITLY, as the brief requires: the current G7 GDP-weighted 10y real is ~1.9-2.0% (Fermi ~2.0 via an implied-US back-out, Scout ~2.0, Historian ~1.7), roughly 60bp BELOW DFII10, so the aggregate starts near its own long-run mean and my 1.82 central assumes only ~-8bp of reversion over six to ten years. Anchoring on the US TIPS print alone is the modal error here. Against that, the Scout's 13-day path (2.88, 2.84, 2.78, 2.74, 2.80, 2.77, 2.76, 2.64, 2.66, 2.66, 2.64, 2.67, 2.63, 2.60, 2.62) shows a ~26bp rally INTO 30 Oct, so the locked anchor is the low of its own fortnight — mildly supportive of the level. VARIANCE ATTRIBUTION: term premium sigma 0.60 (~52% of variance), r* sigma 0.38 (~21%), repression wedge sigma 0.33 (~16%) but FIRST in left-tail SHAPE because it is bimodal (0 or -75 to -150bp), total sigma ~0.78. The term premium carries most of my variance AND essentially all of the variance correlated with the demographics x AI coupling, because the coupling acts through issuance volume, convenience yield and marginal-buyer fragility — all term-premium objects — whereas r*'s variance turns on a contested life-cycle saving elasticity that aging moves in both directions at once. P10 = 0.80 (below Gaussian for the bimodal wedge: heavy repression, the AI capex bust, a BIS-style demand deficit and a flight-to-quality bid together). P90 = 2.85 (fiscal dominance expressed in YIELDS not caps, a basis-trade unwind, TP past 220bp). MODEL EVIDENCE: the adapter refuses CQ4 outright and my own grep confirms why — no termPremium, no breakeven, no nominal sovereign benchmark anywhere in src/. Its economy-wide real return on capital (5.99-6.96% across ten scenarios, debt-populism highest at 6.955% with a 2.502pp debt risk premium) is a different object; LR ~1.0, adopted for nothing. I note only that its cross-scenario spread (~95bp) is NARROWER than my own P10-P90 (205bp), which is the right relationship. DIRECTION: AMPLIFIED upward by +20bp at the P50 (1.6 -> 1.8), entirely through the term premium (0.75 -> 1.15 at LR 2.5:1), and partly offset by my refusal to double-count the (r-g) claim on the r side. Left tail widened 40bp for the bimodal wedge; right tail cut 15bp.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:58.177Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"The ACM or Kim-Wright US 10y real term premium, as published by the New York Fed, prints BELOW +0.40% as a trailing twelve-month average, while the real 10s30s slope (DFII30 minus DFII10) flattens to under +15bp. This is the direct falsifier of the 2.5:1 likelihood ratio that carries my whole payload: it would say the 2026 duration repricing was a transient selloff of the 2013-14 type rather than a regime, and that my 0.75 -> 1.15 term-premium update was unjustified.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ4 P50 falls from 1.8% toward 1.25-1.35% (the term premium returns to the Historian's +0.75 prior or below, with r* and 